<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#druk-desi">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Druk Desi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1650s–1907</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Druk Desi was the civil ruler of Bhutan under the dual system of government created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a>, the Zhabdrung, in the seventeenth century, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a> headed the monastic body. In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries the office was often fought over by the regional governors (penlops). It was abolished in 1907, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a> became hereditary king.</p></div>
